  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "A 1 metre piece of wire is cut and then bent into two figures: one circle and one square. Find the smallest total area of the two figures.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q14-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "\\dfrac{1}{16+4\\pi}\\text{ m}^2",
                "display": false
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q14-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Express both areas using the length allocated to the square, then find the minimum of their quadratic sum.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $x$ metres of wire form the square, so $1-x$ metres form the circle, with $0<x<1$. The square has side length $\\frac{x}{4}$ metres and the circle has radius $\\frac{1-x}{2\\pi}$ metres.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} A &= \\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)^2+\\pi\\left(\\frac{1-x}{2\\pi}\\right)^2 \\\\ A &= \\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(1-x)^2}{4\\pi} \\\\ A &= \\frac{4+\\pi}{16\\pi}x^2-\\frac{x}{2\\pi}+\\frac{1}{4\\pi}. \\end{align*}$$\n\nThe leading coefficient is positive, so the vertex gives the minimum.\n\n$$\\begin{align*} x &= \\frac{1/(2\\pi)}{2(4+\\pi)/(16\\pi)} \\\\ x &= \\frac{4}{4+\\pi}. \\end{align*}$$\n\nThis lies between 0 and 1. Substituting,\n\n$$\\begin{align*} A_{\\min} &= \\frac{1}{(4+\\pi)^2}+\\frac{\\pi}{4(4+\\pi)^2} \\\\ A_{\\min} &= \\frac{1}{4(4+\\pi)}. \\end{align*}$$\n\nThe smallest total area is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16+4\\pi}\\text{ m}^2}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
